{:edb/db-before-t 1565 :edb/db-after-t 1566 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534878 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:51:32.205Z" 13194139534878 true] [17592186119567 1100 "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(-3,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 0, 2, 0 \\rangle$." 13194139534878 false] [17592186119567 1100 "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(-3,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 0, 2, 0 \\rangle.$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186119567 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,2,0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nis normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf p$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n - p \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ &= ⟨ - 3, 2, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534878 false] [17592186119567 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,2,0 \\rangle$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =⟨-3,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186119568 1100 "A nonzero vector $\\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c} \\rangle$ is parallel to the plane $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle  = 3.$ What is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186119568 1100 "A nonzero vector $\\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c} \\rangle$ is parallel to the plane $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle = 3$. What is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}$?" 13194139534878 false] [17592186119568 1145 "We need a vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nthat is parallel to the given plane.\n\nFrom the equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle = 3\n$$\n\nof the plane, we get the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n⟨t_{1}, t_{2}, t_{3}⟩ \\cdot ⟨2,-1, 4⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n2t_{1} - t_{2} + 4t_{3} &= 0 \\\\\nt_{2} &= 2t_{1} + 4t_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle t_1, 2t_1 + 4t_3, t_3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere $t_1,t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0$. So, we must set $t_1=0$.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any\n\n$$\nt_3 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFor example, setting $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{4}, \\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1} \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{c} = 4\n$$" 13194139534878 false] [17592186119568 1145 "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle  = 3$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle.$ So, $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-1,4⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+4{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =2{t}_{1}+4{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle t_1, 2t_1 + 4t_3, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_1,t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set $t_1=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any $t_3 \\neq 0.$ For example, setting $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{4}, \\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1} \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{b}{c} = 4.$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186119569 1100 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(\\sqrt{2},0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=-1+\\sqrt{3}t \\\\ z=5-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186119569 1100 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(\\sqrt{2},0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n${x = 3; y =-1 + \\sqrt{3}t; z = 5 - t$." 13194139534878 false] [17592186119569 1145 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{q}=\\langle \\sqrt{2},0,-5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nConverting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{x = 3; y =-1 + \\sqrt{3}t; z = 5 - t\n$$\n\nThe direction vector of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 0, \\sqrt{3}, -1\\rangle\n$$\n\nso a normal vector to the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 0, \\sqrt{3}, -1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - q) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n - q \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= q \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{3},-1⟩ &= ⟨\\sqrt{2}, 0,-5⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{3},-1⟩ &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534878 false] [17592186119569 1145 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=\\langle \\sqrt{2},0,-5 \\rangle.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042677/assets/4c70e51d85874e5a241a5d9b35b147500d4c306187c5e0a1d15a48a9aef07706.png)\n\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1\\rangle,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=\\langle  0 , \\sqrt{3} , -1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =⟨\\sqrt{2},0,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186119753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316910 13194139534878 true] [17592186119753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316917 13194139534878 true] [17592186119753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316924 13194139534878 true] [17592186119753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316931 13194139534878 true] [17592186119753 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316938 13194139534878 true] [17592186119754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316945 13194139534878 true] [17592186119754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316952 13194139534878 true] [17592186119754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316959 13194139534878 true] [17592186119754 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316966 13194139534878 true] [17592186119755 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316889 13194139534878 true] [17592186119755 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316896 13194139534878 true] [17592186119755 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316903 13194139534878 true] [17592186119755 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316973 13194139534878 true] [17592186119755 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316980 13194139534878 true] [17592186120435 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534878 true] [17592186120435 1100 "A nonzero vector $⟨a, 0, c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r \\cdot ⟨3,-3, 1⟩ =-12$. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}$?" 13194139534878 false] [17592186120435 1100 "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,0,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨3,-3,1⟩=-12.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186120435 1145 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation of the plane, $r\\cdot ⟨3,-3,1⟩=-12,$  we get the normal vector $n=⟨3,-3,1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-3,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3{t}_{1}-3{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =3{t}_{2}-3{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},3{t}_{2}-3{t}_{1}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ eqauls $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,0,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{1}{-3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316889 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316890 13194139534878 true] [17592186316889 1089 "q-127937" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316889 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534878 true] [17592186316889 1100 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5).$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316889 1113 #uuid "78e0dc1f-5e12-54f0-b00c-492a979933d2" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316889 1145 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,3,5⟩-⟨-4,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨6,2,2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316891 13194139534878 true] [17592186316890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316892 13194139534878 true] [17592186316890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316893 13194139534878 true] [17592186316890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316894 13194139534878 true] [17592186316890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316895 13194139534878 true] [17592186316890 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534878 true] [17592186316890 1041 "selection" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316890 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316893 13194139534878 true] [17592186316890 1110 #uuid "3f9ca81e-819f-5a83-8bcc-efef9d6aa0a5" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316891 1034 "r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩=22" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534878 true] [17592186316891 1111 #uuid "fc8a9931-4b41-5c66-9d98-fcf79c4820c8" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316892 1034 "r\\cdot ⟨2,3,2⟩=14" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316892 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534878 true] [17592186316892 1111 #uuid "347d57a2-fbb7-5583-bb16-786d4e1ae381" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316893 1034 "r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩=16" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316893 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534878 true] [17592186316893 1111 #uuid "013eae6a-093f-5868-8fc4-0d2b8abb0040" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316894 1034 "r\\cdot ⟨3,2,2⟩=8" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316894 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534878 true] [17592186316894 1111 #uuid "7c7607c9-f26a-5689-8932-61774fb7d075" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316895 1034 "r\\cdot ⟨2,6,2⟩=12" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316895 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534878 true] [17592186316895 1111 #uuid "88e38b3b-d0e0-5930-b8b9-dd2485c127b3" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316896 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316897 13194139534878 true] [17592186316896 1089 "q-127944" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316896 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534878 true] [17592186316896 1100 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(-1,1,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5-3t \\\\ y=2+4t \\\\ z=-3+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316896 1113 #uuid "47335439-5d39-5166-8bb4-814a188345e9" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316896 1145 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-1,1,2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5-3t \\\\ y=2+4t \\\\ z=-3+t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨5,2,-3⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-3,4,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-3,4,1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-3,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ & =⟨-1,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316898 13194139534878 true] [17592186316897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316899 13194139534878 true] [17592186316897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316900 13194139534878 true] [17592186316897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316901 13194139534878 true] [17592186316897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316902 13194139534878 true] [17592186316897 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534878 true] [17592186316897 1041 "selection" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316897 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316898 13194139534878 true] [17592186316897 1110 #uuid "c8739ff2-6b64-5141-8eae-d81ba06fd6a7" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316898 1034 "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩=9" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534878 true] [17592186316898 1111 #uuid "8d1083a7-8ac3-5e89-9c34-77320f55a409" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316899 1034 "r\\cdot ⟨1,4,3⟩=11" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534878 true] [17592186316899 1111 #uuid "3eed3c19-8e3e-504d-8d6e-7979b366f1ec" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316900 1034 "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,3⟩=15" 13194139534878 true] [17592186316900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534878 true] [17592186316900 1111 #uuid "24c06ecf-5904-5987-bbad-17a96f